# Numbers, dates and household data

A useful number needs a meaning, a unit and a time context. Keep what the source measured separate from estimates and editorial interpretation. A neat display must not hide uncertainty or change the result.

<h2 id="wg-070">WG-070 — Name the measured quantity</h2>

**MUST.** Identify the quantity and distinguish rates, totals, settings, estimates and reported measurements.

Watts describe power; kilowatt-hours describe energy over a period. A target temperature is not the measured room temperature. An estimated cost is not necessarily a bill or tariff charge. Use the integration’s documented quantity and source metadata; do not infer it solely from a friendly name.

<%= example('wg-data-meaning') %>

<h2 id="wg-071">WG-071 — Preserve meaningful precision</h2>

**MUST.** Do not round, convert or calculate a displayed value in a way that changes its meaning or implies greater accuracy than the source supports.

NHS general-number advice is not a rule to round every sensor to a whole number. Keep the native display precision where supplied. If a summary rounds a value, distinguish it from an exact exported or tabular value when the difference matters. Avoid long decimal strings produced only by floating-point arithmetic, and do not invent decimal places to make a measurement look more precise.

<h2 id="wg-072">WG-072 — Respect number and date preferences</h2>

**MUST.** Use Home Assistant’s configured locale, number format, clock and time-zone preferences for dynamic values.

A person using an English interface may prefer a comma decimal separator. Do not force UK punctuation or a 12-hour clock into native values because this guide uses UK English prose. For static documentation, state the example’s convention and date context; the synthetic examples here use Europe/London where relevant.

<h2 id="wg-073">WG-073 — Keep units attached to their values</h2>

**MUST.** Show the correct unit or a clearly associated column or axis unit, and preserve the distinction between similarly named units.

Use a leading zero for a fractional prose value. Explain an unfamiliar unit near the task, not by replacing its symbol with an incorrect word. Keep kW and kWh distinct. If a unit conversion is offered, perform the conversion correctly and identify the resulting unit; changing only the label is never a content fix.

<h2 id="wg-074">WG-074 — Identify the time represented by data</h2>

**MUST.** Label an observation time, interval start, interval end or selected period according to the actual data model.

Native power points can represent observation or display times; energy bars can represent intervals. State the selected date or range and the relevant zone. A “last changed” time is different from “last reported” or “last updated”. Do not use those labels interchangeably or describe a documentation review date as the time a sensor last reported.

<h2 id="wg-075">WG-075 — Make ambiguous times and schedules explicit</h2>

**MUST.** Explain the relevant time zone and boundaries when a schedule, comparison or repeated clock time could be misunderstood.

A schedule may use the household zone while a viewer uses a different browser zone. State which controls the action. For repeated clock times around daylight-saving changes, include the offset or another unambiguous distinction when needed. Do not claim that every calendar day contains the same number of hourly intervals or that a scheduled action ran solely because its time passed.

<h2 id="wg-076">WG-076 — Preserve missing readings and real zeros</h2>

**MUST.** Show a missing, unknown or unavailable value as missing; preserve a genuine zero and identify incomplete totals.

A blank interval must not become zero consumption. A line must not silently bridge a gap if that implies unobserved readings. If the available data supports only a partial total, label its coverage. For a device group, include unavailable members in the explanation rather than saying “all lights off” based only on available lights.

<%= example('wg-data-gaps') %>

<h2 id="wg-077">WG-077 — Give charts equivalent context and readable data</h2>

**MUST.** Provide a meaningful title, quantity, period and accessible data alternative that describes the same values and uncertainty as the chart.

A table is not equivalent if it advances to a new period while the chart still shows the previous one. Preserve series names and stable identities. State whether the data covers all selected-period values or only a filtered subset. Colour, a legend key or an interactive tooltip alone cannot supply all essential meaning.

<h2 id="wg-078">WG-078 — Make comparisons like for like</h2>

**MUST.** Name both periods, their coverage and any calculation behind a change or saving claim.

“Today so far” and “yesterday” are not equal periods. A percentage decrease is meaningless without a defined baseline and may be undefined when that baseline is zero. Keep energy change separate from money saved, and measured data separate from forecasts. Do not imply causation from a visual correlation.

